    "Binary Search",
    "Sorting"
  ],
  "statement": "The **distance of a pair** of integers `a` and `b` is defined as the absolute difference between `a` and `b`.\n\nGiven an integer array `nums` and an integer `k`, return _the_ `k^(th)` _smallest **distance among all the pairs**_ `nums[i]` _and_ `nums[j]` _where_ `0 <= i < j < nums.length`.\n\n**Example 1:**\n\n```\nInput: nums = [1,3,1], k = 1\nOutput: 0\nExplanation: Here are all the pairs:\n(1,3) -> 2\n(1,1) -> 0\n(3,1) -> 2\nThen the 1st smallest distance pair is (1,1), and its distance is 0.\n```\n\n**Example 2:**\n\n```\nInput: nums = [1,1,1], k = 2\nOutput: 0\n```\n\n**Example 3:**\n\n```\nInput: nums = [1,6,1], k = 3\nOutput: 5\n```\n\n**Constraints:**\n\n*   `n == nums.length`\n*   `2 <= n <= 10^(4)`\n*   `0 <= nums[i] <= 10^(6)`\n*   `1 <= k <= n * (n - 1) / 2`",
  "constraints": [
    "n == nums.length",
    "2 <= n <= 104",
    "0 <= nums[i] <= 106",
    "1 <= k <= n * (n - 1) / 2"
  ],
  "examples": [
